{{Politics of Balisca}}

'''Human rights in Balisca''' protect the [[W:Fundamental rights|fundamental rights]] of all persons within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. These rights are safeguarded by the [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]] the [[Constitution of Balisca]] or ''Leyes Fundamentales'', as well as the ratification of various [[W:International treaties|international treaties]] facilitated by the [[W:United Nations|United Nations]]. The country maintains a dedicated commitment to [[W:Human rights|human rights]].

== Sources of human rights ==

=== The [[W:Constitution|Constitution]] of Balisca ===
{{See also|Constitution of Balisca}}
The [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]] of the Baliscan Constitution pertains specifically to the maintenance of human rights of all people within the confederation. Individual rights are enshrined by various Basic Laws, most notably:

* Right to personal integrity (physical, psychosocial, sexual and affective)
* Prohibition of the death penalty, torture, enforced disappearance, slavery, human trafficking and exile
* Right to equality before the law
* Right to non-discrimination
* Right to progressive autonomy of children and adolescents
* Right to the full development of the personality and the use of one's own language
* Right to child protection
* Right to life and environment free of violence (especially for girls, adolescents and people of sexual and gender diversity and dissidence)
* Right to universal accessibility
* Right to dignified treatment, social reintegration and communication (in case of imprisonment)
* Right to a decent old age
* Right to education (including sex education)
* Right to care for the environment
* Right to freedom of education
* Right to health
* Right to care
* Right to decent housing
* Collective right to the city and territory
* Right to food sovereignty
* Right to indigenous consent
* Right to private freedom of worship
* Right to a dignified death
* Right to freedom of movement
* Right to privacy
* Right to asylum and refuge
* Right to freedom of association
* Right to public demonstration
* Right to access information from the State
* Right to {{W|personal property}}
* Right to {{W|free love}} and civil marriage (all forms)
* Right to indigenous sovereignty and decolonization
* Right to freedom to undertake
* Right to freedom of expression
* Right to digital connectivity, computer security, a digital space free of violence, along with education in the digital space
* Right to personal data protection
* Right to cultural heritage
* Right to regeneration of nature
* Right to a healthy and balanced environment
* Universal access to mountains, beaches, lakes, lagoons and wetlands
* Access to justice and due process

=== Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) ===
{{Main|Universal Declaration of Human Rights}}
After the barbarities of [[W:World War II|World War II]], an international declaration was constructed to guarantee the rights of all humans on earth. Proclaimed by the newly constructed [[W:United Nations|United Nations]], at the General Assembly in [[W:Paris|Paris]] on 10 December 1948, the declaration signposted 30 key human rights that should be universally granted to every human.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url=https://www.un.org/en/universal-declaration-human-rights/|title=Universal Declaration of Human Rights|date=2015-10-06|website=www.un.org|language=en|access-date=2020-02-06}}</ref> Although the Declaration is not legally binding in itself, subsequent international [[W:Treaty|treaties]], [[W:Amendment|amendments]] to constitutions, and economic agreements affirm the declaration.

The [[W:Universal Declaration of Human Rights|Universal Declaration of Human Rights]] (UDHR) is seen as the precursor to the [[W:International Bill of Human Rights|International Bill of Human Rights]], which consists of the UDHR, as well as the [[W:International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights|International Covenant of Civil and Political Rights]] (ICCPR, 1966) and the [[W:International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights|International Covenant on Economic, Social, and Cultural Rights]] (ICESCR, 1996). This extension of the UDHR provided a legal reason for states to ensure the maintenance of human rights, in addition to the moral obligation established by the initial proclamation of the UDHR in 1948.

=== International Treaties ===
As well as the UDHR, and the International Bill of Human Rights, Balisca has ratified a variety of other international treaties focused on the maintenance of human rights:, those include:

* [[W:Genocide Convention|Convention of the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide]] (1948)
* [[W:Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees|Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees]] (1951)
* [[W:Convention on the Political Rights of Women|Convention on the Political Rights of Women]] (1952)
* [[W:Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons|Convention Relating to the Status of Stateless Persons]] (1954)
* [[W:Supplementary Convention on the Abolition of Slavery|Supplementary Convention on the Abolition of Slavery]] (1956)
* [[W:Convention on the Nationality of Married Women|Convention on the Nationality of Married Women]] (1957)
* [[W:Convention on the Reduction of Statelessness|Convention on the Reduction of Statelessness]] (1961)
* [[W:Convention on Consent to Marriage, Minimum Age for Marriage and Registration of Marriages|Convention on Consent to Marriage, Minimum Age for Marriage and Registration of Marriages]] (1962)
* [[W:International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination|International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination]] (1965)
* [[W:International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights|International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights]] (1966)
* [[W:International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights|International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights]] (1966)
* [[W:First Optional Protocol to the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights|First Optional Protocol to the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights]] (1967)
* [[W:Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women|Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women]] (1979)

